# Beethoven, Symphony No. 9, finale: the "Freude" tune as the cellos and basses first play
# it alone, Allegro assai, bars 92-115 of the first edition (Schott, 1826, plate 2322,
# printed pages 101-102). The edition writes the tune's repeated notes in bars 1, 3, 5, 7
# and 15 as half notes (F#h G A, not F# F# G A); they are kept that way here.
# The tune is written an octave above the cellos, where the violins take it from bar 140.
# Bar 115 ends with an upbeat A into the next statement; here the tune ends on D, as in
# bars 99 and 107.
# Left hand (Advanced): the double-bass line of the next statement (bars 116-138), where
# the violas play the same tune, at the basses' sounding pitch. The chords are our reading
# of that bass line under the tune; the orchestra has no chord parts here.
title: Ode to Joy
piece: ode-to-joy
added: 2026-10-17
source: public-domain
composer: Ludwig van Beethoven
composer-died: 1827
first-published: 1826
edition: First edition, B. Schott's Söhne, Mainz, n.d. [1826], plate 2322 (IMSLP #46254)
key: D major
meter: 4/4
# The edition marks "Allegro assai"; the tempo is ours.
tempo: 112
summary: Beethoven's tune from the Ninth, as the cellos and basses first play it, over the bass line that follows.
figure: held
# Every bar with a dotted rhythm or the eighth-note turn stays as written in Easy.
easy-keep: 4, 8, 10, 11, 12, 16, 18, 19, 20, 24

